-- How Instagram Algorithm Works | How to Grow on Instagram
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Explain what the ranking systems are actually optimising for', 'Stop treating the algorithm as a single thing to be tricked', 'Decide which signals your own content should aim at'],
  notes = 'There is no single Instagram algorithm. Each surface - the feed, Reels, Stories, Explore - ranks separately and is asking a different question, which is why a post can do well in one place and nowhere else.

WHAT THEY ALL OPTIMISE FOR is the same thing underneath: keeping the person watching and bringing them back tomorrow. Everything else follows from that. The signals that matter most are how long people watch before leaving, whether they watch to the end or again, and whether they send it to someone. A send is the strongest signal available, because it is the platform getting a new visit for free.

SAVES matter for content people intend to use later - recipes, prices, how-to steps. Comments matter less than they are said to, and likes matter least of all, which is the opposite of what most people optimise for.

FOLLOWERS ARE NOT REACH. A post is shown to a small portion of your followers first, and whether it travels further depends on how those people behave. This is why a page with 20,000 bought or irrelevant followers reaches fewer people than a page with 2,000 real ones, and why buying followers damages the only thing that actually matters.

WHAT YOU CANNOT DO IS TRICK IT. Engagement pods, comment-for-comment groups, follow-unfollow and reposting other people''s videos all produce a burst and then a flatter account, because the system measures what happens after the burst.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO - two things that decide this locally. First, most of your audience is watching on a phone on a data bundle, often with the sound off in a matatu or at a desk, so a video that needs sound to make sense loses half its watch time. Put the words on screen. Second, LANGUAGE IS A REACH DECISION. Kiswahili or Sheng reaches a Kenyan audience far better and caps you out of the international one; English reaches wider and competes with everyone. Choose deliberately based on who you want to sell to, because a local audience of 3,000 is worth more to a Nakuru business than 50,000 scattered viewers who cannot buy from them.',
  practice_activity = 'Open your own account insights and find, for your last ten posts, how long people watched and how many sent each one to someone. Rank the ten by sends rather than likes and write down what the top three have in common. Then rewrite one of your worst-performing posts so it makes sense with the sound off.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Which engagement signal is the strongest?", "options": ["A send to someone else, because the platform gets a new visit from it", "A like", "A comment", "A follow"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "Why does buying followers reduce reach?", "options": ["Reach depends on how the first viewers behave, and irrelevant followers behave badly", "Platforms delete purchased accounts immediately", "It lowers the follower count again later", "It blocks the account from Explore"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '0dc0ee83-02fe-42b1-a77a-df3396c3f455';

-- THE TIKTOK ALGORITHM EXPLAINED
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Explain how a video is distributed in stages', 'Understand why follower count matters less here than anywhere else', 'Aim content at the one metric that decides distribution'],
  notes = 'TikTok distributes in stages. A new video is shown to a small batch of people. If they watch it through, rewatch it or share it, it goes to a larger batch, and so on. That is the whole mechanism, and everything else is detail.

THE CONSEQUENCE THAT MATTERS: follower count barely decides anything. An account with 40 followers can reach a hundred thousand people, and an account with 40,000 can post something that reaches nine hundred. This is genuinely different from older platforms, and it is the reason to start here if you are starting from nothing.

WATCH-THROUGH RATE IS THE METRIC - the percentage of the video people actually watch. A twelve-second video watched fully beats a ninety-second video abandoned at fifteen seconds, every time. Shorter is not automatically better; the right length is the shortest one that tells the whole thing.

REWATCHES ARE THE MULTIPLIER. A video short enough and dense enough that people loop it twice counts for far more than one view, which is why a clean loop with no dead air at the end is worth building deliberately.

THE FIRST SECOND decides the rest, which the hooks lesson in Module 3 covers properly.

WHAT DOES NOT WORK: deleting a video because it did badly in the first hour, posting eleven times a day, and chasing every sound. Videos here can be picked up days or weeks later, which does not happen on other platforms, so leave them up.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO - the local reality. UPLOADING VIDEO COSTS MONEY on a bundle, so film in good light the first time to avoid reshoots, edit on the phone, and upload on wifi where you can find it. The feed is also heavily local by default, which is an advantage: content about a Kenyan place, price, queue or problem finds Kenyan viewers quickly with no audience at all. And be clear about income - platform payout programmes are largely unavailable here, so views alone pay nothing. The money comes from brand work, selling your own thing, or running accounts for other businesses, which Module 6 covers.',
  practice_activity = 'Post three videos of the same idea at different lengths - about ten seconds, about twenty-five, about fifty - and compare the percentage watched rather than the views. Then take your best one and re-cut it so the end runs straight back into the beginning, repost it a week later, and compare the rewatch figure.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What decides whether a video is shown to a bigger batch?", "options": ["Whether the small first batch watches through, rewatches or shares it", "How many followers the account has", "How many hashtags were used", "What time of day it was posted"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "What actually pays a Kenyan creator?", "options": ["Brand work, selling your own product, or running accounts for businesses - not platform payouts", "A creator fund, once you pass a follower threshold", "Per-view advertising revenue", "Live gifting alone"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = 'f2f6a7df-beb3-40bc-8c0e-76edf5e59458';

-- Instagram Content Strategy 101 (0 to 100,000+ Followers)
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Build a content plan you can actually sustain', 'Turn a profile into something that converts a visitor', 'Choose formats by what they are for rather than what is fashionable'],
  notes = 'A strategy is what stops you posting whatever occurs to you on the day, which is the reason most accounts stall.

START WITH WHO AND WHY, in one sentence: who is this for, and what do they get from following. If you cannot say it, neither can a visitor deciding whether to follow, and neither can a brand deciding whether to pay you.

CONTENT PILLARS are three or four subjects you will post about repeatedly. A cook might run recipes, cost breakdowns, kitchen mistakes and behind-the-scenes. Pillars remove the daily blank page and make the account legible to someone scrolling your profile for four seconds.

FORMATS HAVE JOBS. Short video reaches people who do not follow you - it is where growth comes from. Carousels and posts teach depth to people who already follow. Stories keep existing followers warm and are where selling actually happens without annoying anyone. Do not use one format for everything.

THE PROFILE IS THE CONVERSION POINT, and it is where most accounts waste their traffic. When a video works, thousands of strangers arrive at a profile that says nothing about what you do or where you are. Name the thing and the place, say who it is for, and make the next step obvious.

CONSISTENCY BEATS FREQUENCY. Three posts a week for a year beats daily for three weeks and then silence, which is what almost everyone actually does.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO - make the profile work for a local business, because that is where the money is here. Put the town in the bio. Make the WhatsApp number the main call to action rather than a website nobody will load on a bundle. Pin three posts that answer the questions people always ask: what you sell, what it costs, and how delivery works. And batch your filming, because one session of good light on a Saturday produces a fortnight of content, which matters when you are doing this around a job and paying for your own data.',
  practice_activity = 'Write your one sentence - who this is for and what they get. Choose three pillars and list five post ideas under each, so you have fifteen without thinking again. Then rewrite your bio to name the thing and the town, set WhatsApp as the action, and pin three posts answering what you sell, what it costs and how delivery works.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Which format brings in people who do not already follow you?", "options": ["Short video", "Stories", "Carousels", "Pinned posts"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "Where do most accounts waste the traffic from a video that worked?", "options": ["The profile, which says nothing about what they do or where", "The caption", "The hashtags", "The posting time"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = 'f7a81270-b26f-4e95-8a49-7773f897771c';

-- THE ULTIMATE TIKTOK TUTORIAL FOR BEGINNERS
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Set up an account and film a first video with what you already own', 'Use sounds, captions and cover frames the way the platform expects', 'Publish without waiting to own better equipment'],
  notes = 'This is the mechanics lesson, and the most useful thing in it is that you can start today with the phone you have.

THE ACCOUNT: a username people can spell and say aloud, a photograph where your face or product is recognisable at thumbnail size, and a bio saying what you do and where. Switch to a business or creator account so you can see the numbers, because Module 4 depends on them.

FILMING WITH WHAT YOU HAVE. Light is the whole difference between amateur and professional, and daylight is free. Face a window, or shoot outside in the morning or late afternoon, and never with a window behind you. Brace the phone against something solid. Record in a room with soft things in it rather than a bare one that echoes, and get close to the phone when you speak.

SOUNDS matter for reach, and the rule is simple: use one that is already circulating if it fits, and do not force it if it does not. A video that makes sense without the sound survives being watched muted, which is most viewing.

CAPTIONS ON SCREEN are not optional. Generate them, then read every line and fix it - names and local words come out wrong every time, and a wrong caption is exactly what viewers notice.

THE COVER FRAME is what people see when they land on your profile, so choose it deliberately instead of letting it pick a frame of you blinking.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO - practical realities here. Shoot upright, never landscape, and hold the phone with two hands or use a clamp costing a few hundred shillings; that plus daylight is the entire equipment budget you need. Clean the lens, which sounds trivial and is the commonest reason a phone video looks hazy. Watch your storage, because a full phone stops a shoot dead. And do not wait for a ring light, a microphone or a better handset - the accounts with real audiences here were built on the phone the person already had, and the equipment argument is almost always a way of postponing the first post.',
  practice_activity = 'Set up or fix your account: a spellable name, a recognisable photograph, a bio naming what you do and the town, switched to a creator or business account. Then film and post one video today using daylight only. Add captions, correct every wrong word, and choose the cover frame deliberately.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What makes the largest difference to how professional a phone video looks?", "options": ["Light - facing a window or shooting in daylight", "The phone model", "An external microphone", "The editing app"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "Why are on-screen captions essential?", "options": ["Most viewing happens with the sound off", "The platform requires them", "They make the video longer", "They remove the need for a hook"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '67db3b45-ec8f-4161-8982-67b0a061c6f9';

-- 7 Reel Types the Instagram Algorithm Currently Favors
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Recognise the durable content shapes behind any list of favoured formats', 'Choose a shape that fits what you are actually selling', 'Judge a trend claim before rebuilding your account around it'],
  notes = 'Treat any list of formats the algorithm currently favours with care. The word doing the work is CURRENTLY. Lists like this are accurate for a season and wrong within a year, and people who rebuild a whole account around one are left stranded when it turns.

WHAT IS DURABLE UNDERNEATH THE LIST - the shapes that have worked for years and will keep working, because they match how people watch:

THE TRANSFORMATION. A before and its after. Works because the first frame poses a question the last frame answers.

THE HOW-TO IN ONE SHOT. A single useful thing done start to finish, short enough to watch twice.

THE MISTAKE. What people get wrong and what to do instead. Works because people watch to check whether they are the one making it.

THE LIST. Three or five of something, counted on screen, so the viewer stays for the one they have not heard.

THE BEHIND-THE-SCENES. How the thing is actually made, priced or delivered. The most underused shape by small businesses, and it builds more trust than anything polished.

THE STORY. Something that happened, told in sequence, with the outcome withheld.

THE ANSWER. Taking a real question, comment or complaint and answering it properly.

MATCH THE SHAPE TO THE GOAL. Transformations suit anything visual - hair, nails, furniture, painting, bodywork. Behind-the-scenes suits anything people are suspicious of, which locally means food, secondhand goods, delivery, and anything paid for in advance.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO - the test to apply to any trend claim before acting on it. Search the format and see whether accounts YOUR SIZE are getting reach from it, not accounts with two million followers whose results prove nothing about yours. And prefer shapes you can repeat weekly with what you already have, because a format needing a location, three people and a day of filming will be abandoned by the third attempt, which is worse than never starting it.',
  practice_activity = 'Take your own subject and write one video idea in each of the seven shapes. Mark which three you could film this week with what you already own, and film one of them. Then find two accounts roughly your size in your subject and note which shapes are actually getting them reach.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What is the problem with a list of currently favoured formats?", "options": ["It is accurate for a season, and rebuilding an account around it leaves you stranded when it turns", "The formats are usually too difficult to film", "Such lists are never accurate", "Format does not affect reach at all"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "Which shape builds most trust for a business people are wary of paying in advance?", "options": ["Behind-the-scenes - how it is actually made, priced and delivered", "The transformation", "The list", "The answer"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = 'f3fd092a-62d6-484f-ad94-a7849b29b9df';

-- Writing TikTok Hooks That Stop the Scroll
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Write a first line that earns the next three seconds', 'Use hook shapes instead of guessing', 'Test hooks against your own retention figures'],
  notes = 'The hook is the first second or two, and it decides everything that follows. A useful video with a weak opening is watched by nobody, which feels unfair and is simply how the mechanism works.

A HOOK IS THREE THINGS AT ONCE: what is said, what is on screen as text, and what is visually happening. All three land in the first second and all three should point the same way. Most people write the spoken line and forget the other two.

SHAPES THAT WORK, none of which require being dishonest:
THE PROBLEM NAMED EXACTLY - the viewer''s specific problem in their own words, which stops the right person even if it stops nobody else.
THE CONTRADICTION - something against what they assume, which they stay to check.
THE RESULT FIRST - showing the finished thing before explaining it.
THE SPECIFIC NUMBER - a real figure, because specificity reads as true.
THE DIRECT ADDRESS - naming exactly who this is for in the first three words.
THE OPEN LOOP - beginning something the viewer needs the end of.

WHAT KILLS A HOOK: greeting people, introducing yourself, explaining what the video is going to be about, apologising, and a slow establishing shot. Nobody has agreed to listen yet. Start in the middle.

DO NOT WRITE A HOOK THE VIDEO DOES NOT DELIVER. It works once and costs you the watch-through on everything after, because people who feel tricked leave faster next time and the system is measuring exactly that.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO - the practical method. Write five hooks for every video and pick the sharpest, because the first one you think of is almost always the flattest. Use the real words your audience uses, including Sheng or Kiswahili where that is how the problem is genuinely said, since a hook in the words people think in stops them faster than a translated one. And test properly: post the same video twice, weeks apart, with different opening lines, and compare the percentage still watching at three seconds. That figure, not the view count, tells you whether the hook worked.',
  practice_activity = 'Take one video you have already made and write five different hooks for it using five different shapes. Choose the sharpest, re-cut the video with it, and post. Note the percentage still watching at three seconds before and after, and keep both numbers.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What are the three parts of a hook?", "options": ["What is said, the on-screen text, and what is visually happening", "The caption, the hashtags and the sound", "The title, the cover and the description", "The opening, the middle and the ending"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "Why is a hook the video does not deliver costly?", "options": ["People who feel tricked leave faster next time, and the system measures exactly that", "The platform removes the video", "The hook cannot be reused", "It reduces the follower count"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '0ea32298-1165-4261-8ea7-967cb760a03d';
